    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(7))  # F0..F6\n    customers = list(range(5))  # C0..C4\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(60, 42), (77, 28), (79, 63), (37, 73), (1, 28), (18, 45), (79, 4)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(58, 12), (89, 34), (98, 64), (66, 81), (67, 75)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [82.0, 125.0, 119.0, 89.0, 99.0, 185.0, 171.0]\n    customer_demands = [37.0, 14.0, 21.0, 37.0, 19.0]\n    fixed_costs = [354.0, 293.0, 271.0, 423.0, 463.0, 194.0, 381.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse opening and customer shipping plan to minimize total cost\n\nWe need to make a near-term decision on which candidate warehouse sites to open and how to ship products to our customers so we can meet demand at the lowest possible total cost.\n\n- Our candidate warehouse locations are specified as map coordinates in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (60, 42)\n  - F1: (77, 28)\n  - F2: (79, 63)\n  - F3: (37, 73)\n  - F4: (1, 28)\n  - F5: (18, 45)\n  - F6: (79, 4). Our customer delivery points are specified as map coordinates in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (58, 12)\n  - C1: (89, 34)\n  - C2: (98, 64)\n  - C3: (66, 81)\n  - C4: (67, 75). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n- Each warehouse has a finite handling capacity; these limits are provided in [82.0, 125.0, 119.0, 89.0, 99.0, 185.0, 171.0]. Each customer has a demand requirement that must be fully satisfied; those quantities are provided in [37.0, 14.0, 21.0, 37.0, 19.0].\n- We can only ship from a warehouse if we decide to open it. Opening any warehouse incurs a fixed cost, detailed in [354.0, 293.0, 271.0, 423.0, 463.0, 194.0, 381.0].\n- Shipping cost scales with both distance and volume moved. The per-distance, per-unit transport rate is 0.5.\n\nThe decision in front of us is to select which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments from the open warehouses to customers so that:\n- Every customer\u2019s demand is met in full,\n- No warehouse exceeds its capacity, and\n- Our total spend (facility opening costs plus transportation costs) is minimized.\n\nPractically, this is a trade-off: opening more sites typically shortens average shipping distances and reduces transport spend, but increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites lowers fixed costs but can raise transportation costs and strain capacity. The outcome we are aiming for is a clear recommendation on which warehouses to activate and a shipment plan that maps customers to the appropriate open sites, with all distances measured as straight-line (Euclidean) distances based on the provided coordinates.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse opening and customer shipping plan to minimize total cost\n\nWe need to make a near-term decision on which candidate warehouse sites to open and how to ship products to our customers so we can meet demand at the lowest possible total cost.\n\n- Our candidate warehouse locations are specified as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}. Our customer delivery points are specified as map coordinates in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n- Each warehouse has a finite handling capacity; these limits are provided in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}. Each customer has a demand requirement that must be fully satisfied; those quantities are provided in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n- We can only ship from a warehouse if we decide to open it. Opening any warehouse incurs a fixed cost, detailed in {FIXED_COSTS}.\n- Shipping cost scales with both distance and volume moved. The per-distance, per-unit transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n\nThe decision in front of us is to select which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments from the open warehouses to customers so that:\n- Every customer\u2019s demand is met in full,\n- No warehouse exceeds its capacity, and\n- Our total spend (facility opening costs plus transportation costs) is minimized.\n\nPractically, this is a trade-off: opening more sites typically shortens average shipping distances and reduces transport spend, but increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites lowers fixed costs but can raise transportation costs and strain capacity. The outcome we are aiming for is a clear recommendation on which warehouses to activate and a shipment plan that maps customers to the appropriate open sites, with all distances measured as straight-line (Euclidean) distances based on the provided coordinates."
}